<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muslim conquest of Persia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/633">633</a>–<a href="/years/654">654</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Arab conquest of Iran, Arab conquest of Persia · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> was the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> between 633 and the early 650s. Arab victories at al-Qadisiyyah (636) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a> (642) opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> to the invaders, and the death of the last Sasanian king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a>, in 651 ended the dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s gradual conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> followed over the next centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia.jpg" alt="Muslim conquest of Persia" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Javierfv1212 and edited by HistoryofIran. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IslamicConquestsIroon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the early 7th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> was under strain. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-sasanian-war-of-602-628">Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628</a> had exhausted its army, and after the execution of the shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> in 628 ten claimants were enthroned within four years. A civil war, the Sasanian Interregnum, left the government decentralized by 632. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> came to the throne aged about eight and did not rebuild the army. The empire was in effect a confederation with Parthian families, some of whom wished to withdraw, and the northern and eastern houses later made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> rather than fight beside the Sasanians.</p><p>Muhammad died in June 632 and Abu Bakr became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a> kept the caliphate occupied until March 633, after which the Arab chief al-Muthanna ibn Haritha raided Sasanian towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> with light cavalry, and his success led Abu Bakr to consider expansion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Under Caliph Umar the Muslims lost ground in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with half his army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> defeated Abu Ubaid al-Thaqafi in the Battle of the Bridge. Al-Muthanna won at Buwayb, and in 635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> sought an alliance with the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>. By 638 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> valleys were under Muslim control, with the Persians withdrawn east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> the Persian chief Hormuzan signed and broke two treaties before he was defeated and captured at Tuster.</p><p>Umar was wary of attacking what remained of the empire, but a new offensive followed, and the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a> in 642 proved the key to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Yazdegerd fled to raise another army with limited success. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> fell after a siege of a few months, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> was taken in stages, and expeditions went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sakastan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. In 651 Ahnaf ibn Qais took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, where Yazdegerd had held court; the remainder of his army was beaten at the Battle of Oxus River and retreated across the Oxus. Umar was assassinated in November 644 by an Iranian slave, Abu Lu'lu'a Firuz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The conquerors settled in garrison towns. Non-Muslim subjects were tolerated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmi</a> and paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, and mass conversion was neither wanted nor allowed in the first centuries. Conversion was slow and incomplete, stretching over many centuries, with a majority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> still following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> at the turn of the millennium. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> stayed the official language under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf">Al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</a> later changed the administrative language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to Arabic. Persian survived as New Persian, with many Arabic words and the Arabic alphabet.</p><p>Historians have long relied on Arabic accounts written well after the events and on the Armenian bishop Sebeos. Parvaneh Pourshariati, in a 2008 study, questions the traditional narrative, including its timeline and specific dates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muslim conquest of Persia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Arab conquest of Iran</b> between 633 and 651 ended Zoroastrianism’s position as the religion of the state.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Arab conquest of Iran</b>, completed by 651, made Arabic the language of religion, government and learning.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
